WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 2, Mathematics and statistics. You do not need a proof-heavy math degree. You need enough fluency to read a model equation, spot a shape bug, understand a loss curve, and reason about uncertainty without pretending a single score is the truth. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Vectors, matrices, and the dot product. Rows are feature vectors and datasets are matrices. These structures make weighted predictions and transformations efficient. The question for this lesson: What shape is the information flowing through?. A dataset is a spreadsheet. A feature vector is one row. A matrix is the whole spreadsheet. That is most of the linear algebra you need on day one, and it is enough to debug a surprising number of shape errors. The dot product is the star of the show: it multiplies matching entries and adds them up. In ML it is a weighted vote. Each feature casts a vote, each weight says how much that vote counts, and the total is the score. You will learn: A dot product is a weighted sum., Matrix multiplication applies the same transformation to many rows., Scale changes distances and the geometry of optimization.. Practice: "Calculate dot products. Watch out for: Losing track of dimensions., Mixing up rows and columns., Ignoring scale.. You are ready when: Know the shapes of X, y, weights, predictions., Explain the dot product., Standardize when scale matters.. Recommended video: "Vectors — Essence of Linear Algebra. Lesson 2: Statistics and uncertainty. Statistics separates stable signal from sampling noise. Learn base rates, distributions, intervals, and why one metric is never the full story. The question for this lesson: How certain should you be about a score?. A single number is a story with the ending missing. A model that scores 0.84 on one split might score 0.79 or 0.88 on the next, and the difference may have nothing to do with the model. The mean is a summary, not a truth. The median laughs at outliers. The variance tells you how much the mean is hiding. And the base rate — how common the positive class is — changes what almost every metric means. You will learn: A mean hides skew and subgroups., Base rates change how metrics should be read., Repeated splits show metric variation instead of a single lucky number.. Practice: Bootstrap a confidence interval for your metric and compare performance across meaningful user slices.. Watch out for: Reporting one score with no spread., Using accuracy on rare positives., Reading correlation as cause.. You are ready when: Inspect distributions., Check prevalence., Report variation.. Recommended video: "The Central Limit Theorem. Lesson 3: Gradient descent. A loss measures error; a gradient measures how parameters change that error. Optimization moves parameters in the direction that reduces loss. The question for this lesson: How does a model learn from error?. Imagine hiking in fog. You cannot see the valley, but you can feel the slope under your feet. Take a step downhill. Repeat. That is gradient descent, minus the boots and the bad decisions. The loss is your altitude. The gradient is the direction of steepest uphill, so you walk the other way. The learning rate is your stride: too small and you crawl, too large and you leap past the valley into a worse one. You will learn: Loss turns many errors into one objective., The gradient is local sensitivity for each parameter., Learning rate can converge, crawl, or diverge.. Practice: "Implement linear regression in NumPy and compare loss curves for a small. Watch out for: Never plotting the loss., Ignoring feature scale., Equating lower training loss with better generalization.. You are ready when: Plot train and validation loss., Check divergence., Explain one update.. Recommended video: "Gradient descent. You can read a model equation and predict how it will behave. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
